🚨 TOP STORY: 🇺🇸🇮🇷 OIL REMAINS ABOVE $100 AS HORMUZ CRISIS CONTINUES
Oil prices remain highly volatile as the conflict involving Iran continues to disrupt energy shipments.
Brent crude briefly moved above $108 a barrel before falling back to around $104, while U.S. crude moved around the $100 mark. Prices remain more than 7% higher for the week. �
Reuters +1
There are now reports of efforts by Middle Eastern countries to find a temporary arrangement that could improve the movement of ships through the Strait of Hormuz, but no lasting agreement has been confirmed. �
Reuters
The International Energy Agency has also sharply reduced its outlook for oil-demand growth, warning that prolonged disruption could keep the global energy market under pressure into 2027. �
Financial Times
🇺🇸 UNITED STATES — WAR'S ECONOMIC IMPACT GROWS
The conflict is increasingly becoming an economic issue for the United States and the wider global economy.
U.S. diesel prices have reportedly passed $6 per gallon for the first time, while rising energy costs are adding to inflation concerns. �
Reuters
Markets are therefore watching the Federal Reserve closely as policymakers face the difficult combination of energy-driven inflation and pressure on economic growth.
🇳🇬 NIGERIA — DANGOTE SECURES 16 MILLION BARRELS OF CRUDE
Nigeria's Dangote refinery has secured at least 16 million barrels of crude oil for October delivery, equivalent to roughly 520,000 barrels per day.
That represents a substantial portion of the refinery's 700,000-barrel-per-day capacity and is considerably higher than its average intake last year. �
Reuters
The development is significant for Nigeria because stronger domestic refining could reduce reliance on imported refined petroleum products. However, the refinery's increased domestic crude purchases also mean less Nigerian crude may be available for export at a time when global oil supplies are already tight. �
Reuters
🌍 AFRICA — ENERGY SECURITY BECOMES A BIGGER ISSUE
The disruption in Middle Eastern energy supplies is creating a difficult environment for African economies.
Oil-producing countries such as Nigeria could benefit from stronger crude prices through increased export earnings, but higher international fuel and transportation costs can also put pressure on consumers and businesses.
The coming months will therefore be important for how African governments balance energy revenues with the cost-of-living impact.
🇺🇦 UKRAINE — RUSSIAN ATTACKS CONTINUE
Russian attacks on Ukrainian cities and infrastructure remain a major concern.
Recent strikes have caused civilian casualties, including deaths in Pavlohrad and Dnipro, while Ukraine continues to face pressure on its energy and other critical infrastructure ahead of another difficult winter. �
The Guardian +1
NATO countries are also increasingly concerned about the security of undersea cables and other critical infrastructure in Europe. �
The Guardian
